<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#pashto">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pashto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pashto language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pashto is an Eastern Iranian language spoken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, written in a modified Arabic script. It is one of the two official languages of Afghanistan. Its literary tradition dates back at least to the 16th century, with poets such as Khushal Khan Khattak and Rahman Baba.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pashto.jpg" alt="Pashto" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Syed Wamiq Ahmed Hashmi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pashto.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
